1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,638
Two business owners.

2
00:00:01,678 --> 00:00:06,113
Same kind of work, same one hundred eighty thousand dollars in profit.

3
00:00:06,153 --> 00:00:10,651
One of them pays about fifteen thousand dollars more in tax every year.

4
00:00:10,691 --> 00:00:15,062
Not because of a loophole, and not because anyone did anything shady.

5
00:00:15,102 --> 00:00:18,321
The only difference is how their business is taxed.

6
00:00:18,361 --> 00:00:25,840
In this video, we'll walk through the actual math, line by line, so you can see exactly where that number comes from.

7
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:32,328
This is Dollars and Deductions.

8
00:00:32,368 --> 00:00:39,671
Today: S corp versus LLC, where that fifteen thousand dollars comes from, and who it actually applies to.

9
00:00:39,711 --> 00:00:41,419
One note before we start.

10
00:00:41,459 --> 00:00:43,797
This is education, not tax advice.

11
00:00:43,837 --> 00:00:51,320
Your numbers will be different, so run your own situation by a tax professional before you change anything.

12
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:55,577
First, a mix-up that trips up a lot of people.

13
00:00:55,617 --> 00:00:59,992
An LLC and an S corp are not two versions of the same thing.

14
00:01:00,032 --> 00:01:03,891
An LLC is a legal structure you form under state law.

15
00:01:03,931 --> 00:01:06,466
It's about ownership and liability.

16
00:01:06,506 --> 00:01:08,378
An S corp is a tax status.

17
00:01:08,418 --> 00:01:16,324
It's an election you make with the IRS that changes how the business's profit is taxed at the federal level.

18
00:01:16,364 --> 00:01:20,297
That means an LLC can choose to be taxed as an S corp.

19
00:01:20,337 --> 00:01:23,901
So the real question usually isn't LLC or S corp.

20
00:01:23,941 --> 00:01:29,753
It's whether your LLC keeps its default tax treatment, or elects S corp status.

21
00:01:34,733 --> 00:01:36,580
Let's start with the default.

22
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:43,866
If you're the only owner, the IRS treats a single-member LLC like a sole proprietorship for income tax purposes.

23
00:01:43,906 --> 00:01:47,119
All of the profit flows onto your personal return.

24
00:01:47,159 --> 00:01:52,128
You pay regular income tax on it, and you also pay self-employment tax on it.

25
00:01:52,168 --> 00:01:58,413
Self-employment tax is how people who work for themselves pay into Social Security and Medicare.

26
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:03,289
The rate is fifteen point three percent.

27
00:02:03,329 --> 00:02:09,217
Twelve point four percent goes to Social Security, and two point nine percent goes to Medicare.

28
00:02:09,257 --> 00:02:12,961
If that sounds high, it's because you're paying both halves.

29
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:19,077
When you work for someone else, your employer pays half of those taxes and you pay the other half.

30
00:02:19,117 --> 00:02:22,673
When you work for yourself, you're both, so you pay both.

31
00:02:26,933 --> 00:02:28,979
Two details matter for the math.

32
00:02:29,019 --> 00:02:33,347
First, self-employment tax isn't charged on every dollar of profit.

33
00:02:33,387 --> 00:02:37,128
It's charged on ninety-two point three five percent of it.

34
00:02:37,168 --> 00:02:43,126
That adjustment roughly mirrors the employer's share you'd be able to deduct as an employee.

35
00:02:43,166 --> 00:02:44,560
Second, there's a cap.

36
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:53,492
The Social Security part only applies up to a yearly limit called the wage base, which the Social Security Administration sets each year.

37
00:02:53,532 --> 00:02:55,813
Our example stays under that limit.

38
00:02:57,467 --> 00:02:58,393
Now the S corp.

39
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:05,094
When your business is taxed as an S corp and you work in it, you become an employee of your own company.

40
00:03:05,134 --> 00:03:08,123
The company runs payroll and pays you a salary.

41
00:03:08,163 --> 00:03:18,948
That salary gets the same Social Security and Medicare taxes as any paycheck: fifteen point three percent in total, half paid by the company and half withheld from you.

42
00:03:18,988 --> 00:03:23,845
Profit left over after your salary can be paid out to you as a distribution.

43
00:03:23,885 --> 00:03:28,267
Distributions aren't wages, so they don't carry those payroll taxes.

44
00:03:33,967 --> 00:03:35,698
Here's what doesn't change.

45
00:03:35,738 --> 00:03:41,077
You still pay income tax on all of the business's profit, the salary and the rest.

46
00:03:41,117 --> 00:03:46,784
An S corp passes its profit through to your personal return, just like the default LLC.

47
00:03:46,824 --> 00:03:50,392
So the difference we're talking about isn't income tax.

48
00:03:50,432 --> 00:03:51,507
It's payroll tax.

49
00:03:51,547 --> 00:04:00,207
The S corp charges Social Security and Medicare only on the part you pay yourself as salary, instead of on nearly all of the profit.

50
00:04:04,067 --> 00:04:06,679
And here's the catch everyone asks about.

51
00:04:06,719 --> 00:04:11,661
Why not pay yourself a tiny salary and take everything else as distributions?

52
00:04:11,701 --> 00:04:18,520
Because the IRS requires S corp owners who work in the business to pay themselves reasonable compensation.

53
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:24,343
That means roughly what the business would have to pay someone else to do the work you do.

54
00:04:24,383 --> 00:04:31,071
The IRS looks at things like your role, your hours, your experience, and what comparable businesses pay.

55
00:04:31,111 --> 00:04:33,724
There's no fixed percentage in the rules.

56
00:04:33,764 --> 00:04:43,987
Underpaying yourself to avoid payroll taxes is exactly what the IRS watches for, and it can treat those distributions as wages, with back taxes and penalties.

57
00:04:46,967 --> 00:04:47,345
Okay.

58
00:04:47,345 --> 00:04:51,015
Let's do the math with one example, step by step.

59
00:04:51,055 --> 00:04:55,447
Same business, same work, same profit, two tax treatments.

60
00:04:56,367 --> 00:05:01,277
Our owner's business makes one hundred eighty thousand dollars in profit for the year.

61
00:05:01,317 --> 00:05:05,807
That's after business expenses, and before the owner pays themselves anything.

62
00:05:07,267 --> 00:05:14,113
Under the default LLC treatment, self-employment tax starts with ninety-two point three five percent of the profit.

63
00:05:14,153 --> 00:05:18,064
That's one hundred sixty-six thousand, two hundred thirty dollars.

64
00:05:18,104 --> 00:05:26,527
Multiply that by fifteen point three percent, and the self-employment tax comes to about twenty-five thousand, four hundred thirty-three dollars.

65
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:29,144
Now the S corp version.

66
00:05:29,184 --> 00:05:34,566
Let's say a reasonable salary for this owner's role is sixty-eight thousand dollars.

67
00:05:34,606 --> 00:05:37,600
We're using that number to illustrate the math.

68
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:40,956
A real reasonable salary depends on the actual work.

69
00:05:40,996 --> 00:05:46,378
Payroll taxes on that salary, both halves together, are fifteen point three percent.

70
00:05:46,418 --> 00:05:49,411
That's ten thousand, four hundred four dollars.

71
00:05:49,451 --> 00:05:56,100
Most of the remaining profit comes out as distributions, with no Social Security or Medicare tax on it.

72
00:05:57,800 --> 00:05:59,057
Put them side by side.

73
00:05:59,097 --> 00:06:03,009
About twenty-five thousand, four hundred dollars under the default.

74
00:06:03,049 --> 00:06:05,958
About ten thousand, four hundred under the S corp.

75
00:06:05,998 --> 00:06:08,848
The gap is about fifteen thousand dollars a year.

76
00:06:08,888 --> 00:06:11,720
That's where the number in the title comes from.

77
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:16,477
Now, the honest part.

78
00:06:16,517 --> 00:06:21,741
That fifteen thousand is the payroll tax difference, not money in your pocket.

79
00:06:21,781 --> 00:06:23,765
Running an S corp costs money.

80
00:06:23,805 --> 00:06:27,814
You need payroll, with quarterly filings and year-end forms.

81
00:06:27,854 --> 00:06:34,495
The business files its own tax return, Form eleven twenty S, which usually means paying a preparer.

82
00:06:34,535 --> 00:06:38,747
Your salary also picks up federal and state unemployment taxes.

83
00:06:38,787 --> 00:06:42,661
And some states charge their own fees or taxes on S corps.

84
00:06:42,701 --> 00:06:49,180
Depending on where you live and who you hire, those costs can run a few thousand dollars a year.

85
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,977
If those costs come to about three thousand dollars, our owner keeps roughly twelve thousand.

86
00:06:58,017 --> 00:06:59,077
Still real money.

87
00:06:59,117 --> 00:07:00,800
Just not the full fifteen.

88
00:07:02,100 --> 00:07:05,058
A few other things can push the result up or down.

89
00:07:05,098 --> 00:07:12,612
Income tax can shift, because of how the qualified business income deduction works when part of your profit is paid as salary.

90
00:07:12,652 --> 00:07:16,030
Some retirement plan contributions depend on your salary.

91
00:07:16,070 --> 00:07:24,784
And at higher profit levels, the Social Security wage base caps the self-employment tax, so the gap stops growing the way it does in this example.

92
00:07:24,824 --> 00:07:31,060
This comparison is payroll tax only, which is why the full picture is worth running with a professional.

93
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,367
So who does this tend to make sense for?

94
00:07:39,407 --> 00:07:45,024
Generally, owners whose profit is well above what a reasonable salary for their role would be.

95
00:07:45,064 --> 00:07:48,995
That gap between profit and salary is where the savings come from.

96
00:07:49,035 --> 00:08:03,960
It also tends to fit owners whose profit is steady from year to year, who are willing to run payroll properly and keep the paperwork clean, and who plan to keep the business going long enough for the savings to outweigh the setup and running costs.

97
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:13,067
And who it usually doesn't fit.

98
00:08:13,107 --> 00:08:24,268
If your profit is close to what a reasonable salary would be, there's not much left to take as distributions, so there's little to save, and the extra costs can wipe it out.

99
00:08:24,308 --> 00:08:32,361
If your business is brand new, or your income swings a lot, you could be paying for payroll in years with very little profit.

100
00:08:32,401 --> 00:08:36,828
A small side business with modest profit often won't clear the costs.

101
00:08:36,868 --> 00:08:41,660
And if you value simplicity, the default setup means a lot less paperwork.

102
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:50,599
If you remember one thing from this video, make it this.

103
00:08:50,639 --> 00:08:54,651
The S corp question isn't really about the label on your business.

104
00:08:54,691 --> 00:09:03,494
It's about the gap between your profit and a reasonable salary for the work you do, and whether that gap is big enough to cover the extra costs.

105
00:09:03,534 --> 00:09:13,360
And if an owner does decide to make the election, it's done on IRS Form twenty-five fifty-three, which has its own deadlines for when the election takes effect.

106
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:25,902
If you want to try this with your own numbers, there's a free calculator on dollarsanddeductions.com.

107
00:09:25,942 --> 00:09:30,538
It runs this same comparison with your profit, your salary, and your costs.

108
00:09:30,578 --> 00:09:35,112
And a reminder: everything in this video is for educational purposes only.

109
00:09:35,152 --> 00:09:37,646
It's not tax, legal, or financial advice.

110
00:09:37,686 --> 00:09:44,980
Tax rules change, and your situation is your own, so talk to a qualified tax professional before you make an election.

111
00:09:48,233 --> 00:09:50,508
Thanks for watching Dollars and Deductions.

112
00:09:50,548 --> 00:09:53,993
If this was useful, subscribe, and I'll see you in the next one.
